1. What licence Simitone is under, and why
================================================================================

Simitone is a derivative work of FreeSO (https://freeso.org,
https://github.com/alexjyong/FreeSO), which Simitone vendors as the FreeSO/
submodule. The solution file builds FreeSO's projects directly, and
Simitone.Windows references FSO.UI, FSO.Common, FSO.Files, FSO.SimAntics,
FSO.LotView, FSO.Content, FSO.HIT, FSO.Vitaboy, FSO.Vitaboy.Engine and the
FSO.SimAntics.JIT compiler. The shipped output therefore contains FSO.Common.dll,
FSO.SimAntics.dll, FSO.LotView.dll, FSO.Files.dll, FSO.UI.dll, FSO.Content.dll,
FSO.Vitaboy.dll and FSO.Vitaboy.Engine.dll.

The default build does NOT reach two more of FreeSO's projects, and that is a
deliberate build split rather than an omission. FSO.Client (The Sims Online's
online client) ProjectReferences the whole TSO *server*, and FSO.IDE (the
"Volcanic" editor) ProjectReferences FSO.Client, so one reference used to pull
nineteen projects into every build - none of which survived into the game, since
the singleplayer output trim deleted them all seconds after producing them. They
are now built only with -p:BuildIde=true, so a stock Simitone.exe contains
neither FSO.Client.dll nor the Volcanic IDE host, and the -ide flag in it throws
a PlatformNotSupportedException naming the switch. The build verifies this: it
reads back the resolved runtime graph in Simitone.deps.json and fails if any of
the twenty-two assemblies the split removed is still in it.

The same split drops one package of FreeSO *content*. FSO.Content.TSO ships
1,405 files (284 MB) of The Sims Online's data - its avatar meshes and
textures, its city, its object table, its client patches, its UI strings and its
UI graphics. Simitone is a The Sims 1 game: it reads the player's own TS1
install at run time and has no use for TSO's data, and the earlier build only
carried those files so that the -ide host could be started. None of it is in a
Simitone build output. The nine content roots are listed, and the drop is
checked, in the _SimitoneContentContract comment in
Client/Simitone/Simitone.Windows/Simitone.Windows.csproj; that same list is why
the split is safe to make at all, because "the game does not need TSO's data"
has to be checked against something rather than believed.

FreeSO is MPL-2.0, and MPL-2.0 is a file-level copyleft licence: a derivative
work that modifies MPL-covered files must keep those files available under
MPL-2.0, and the licence must be made available with any distribution of the
software. Simitone's own code is built on and adapted from those sources, so
there is no way to relicense the result as anything stricter or more permissive
without renegotiating with every FreeSO contributor. Simitone is therefore
released as a whole under MPL-2.0 as well.

The practical consequences, which are the ones that matter to anyone
redistributing a build:

  * LICENSE (MPL-2.0) ships next to the executable. Simitone.Windows.csproj
    copies it, and this NOTICE, into the build output, so a release zip is
    self-describing without reference to this repository.
  * Modified MPL-covered source files remain available. If you take the
    Simitone source and the FreeSO source, your modified versions of those
    files stay under MPL-2.0 and you owe recipients those sources.
  * New Simitone-only files that are not derived from FreeSO could in
    principle carry different terms. They do not: shipping one repository
    under two incompatible licences is a support burden for every downstream
    packager, and it buys nothing that the contributors want. Everything here
    is MPL-2.0 unless a component below says otherwise.

A note on scope: MPL-2.0 obligations attach to the software, not to the
player. Nothing in this file, and nothing in Simitone's build output, grants or
is intended to grant any right in Maxis or EA game assets. See section 3.

